    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagalog-language">Tagalog</a> settlement called Maynila stood on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pasig-river">Pasig River</a> by the 13th century and was conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1571, after which Manila was the capital of the Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">East Indies</a> for about three centuries.</li><li>Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galleon">galleon</a> trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acapulco">Acapulco</a> linked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, and its streets held Chinese, Spanish, Mexican, Japanese and many other peoples, with the walled city of Intramuros at the centre.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> held the city from 1762 to 1764, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> took it in 1898, and in 1945 a battle of about a month destroyed most of it before it was rebuilt as the capital of an independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 1603 Manila was home to 25,000 Chinese, 14,437 native families and 3,528 mixed Spanish-Filipino families, and a Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> wrote that because so many languages met there the confessional in Manila was the most difficult in the world.</p></aside>
    </section>

