    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1258</span><span class="rf-text">A fortified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagalog-language">Tagalog</a> settlement called Maynila is said to exist on the site</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1571</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-lopez-de-legazpi">Miguel López de Legazpi</a> takes Maynila and begins building Spanish Manila on its ruins</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1574</span><span class="rf-text">The Chinese warlord Limahong attacks the city and is defeated</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1645</span><span class="rf-text">An earthquake destroys much of the stone-and-brick city, and another does so in 1677</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1762</span><span class="rf-text">British forces capture Manila and hold it until 1764</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1898</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> cedes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> after the Battle of Manila</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1945</span><span class="rf-text">The Battle of Manila destroys most of the city and civilians are killed in the Manila massacre</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1986</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/people-power-revolution">People Power Revolution</a> ends the rule of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-marcos">Ferdinand Marcos</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/laguna-copperplate-inscription"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/laguna-copperplate-inscription.jpg" alt="Laguna Copperplate Inscription" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/laguna-copperplate-inscription">Laguna Copperplate Inscription</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Laguna_Copperplate_Inscription_in_the_National_Museum_of_Anthropology.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Chipmunkdavis, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Manila stands on the eastern shore of Manila Bay on the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luzon">Luzon</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pasig-river">Pasig River</a> flows through the middle of the city and divides it into northern and southern parts, and almost all of the city sits on prehistoric alluvial deposits built by the Pasig and on land reclaimed from the bay. The earliest evidence of human life near the city is the Angono Petroglyphs, dated to around 3000 BCE. Negritos lived across Luzon before Malayo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesians">Polynesians</a> arrived and assimilated them, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagalog-language">Tagalogs</a> who established the fortified polity of Maynilà were Austronesians. The name is usually read as Maynilà, “where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">indigo</a> plants are abundant”, or as May nilad, “where nilad plants are abundant”, nilad being a shrub-like tree of mangrove swamps.</p><p>The earliest recorded history of the area dates to 900 CE, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laguna-copperplate-inscription">Laguna Copperplate Inscription</a>, which shows that a socially complex Tagalog polity, Tondo, existed on the Pasig delta by then. Archaeological evidence shows that the settlement of Namayan, also called Sapa, flourished further up the Pasig in the tenth or eleventh century. By the thirteenth century the city consisted of a fortified settlement and trading quarter near the mouth of the river. According to the genealogical research of Mariano Henson, a settlement existed in the Maynila area by 1258, ruled by Rajah Avirjirkaya, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a> suzerain, until a Bruneian commander named Rajah Ahmad defeated him and made Maynila a Muslim principality. The traditions of its founding range from the mid-1200s to the early 1600s.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Manila&#8221; cites where it covers May nilà, May nilad, Early history:</p><ul><li>Laura Lee Junker, Raiding, Trading, and Feasting: The Political Economy of Philippine Chiefdoms (Ateneo de Manila University Press, 2000)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Before the Spanish arrived, Maynila and Tondo were active trade partners of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> and Yuan dynasties and flourished in the middle and later periods of the Ming. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> the city became the colonial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/entrepot">entrepôt</a> of the Far East and the western end of the Manila–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acapulco">Acapulco</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galleon">galleon</a> trade, which ran from 1571 until 1815. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">Silver</a> mined in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> was exchanged for Chinese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, Indian gems and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> and Malaysia, and crops of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> such as chilli, papaya and sweet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potato</a> reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> by way of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>. After the Ming ban on trade with the Ashikaga <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> in 1549, Manila became the only place where Japanese and Chinese could openly trade.</p><p>A count of 1603 gives 25,000 Chinese, 14,437 native families and 3,528 mixed Spanish-Filipino families in the city, and the walled city of Intramuros and its suburbs held 1,200 Spanish families and a garrison of 400 Spanish soldiers. The University of Santo <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loma-people">Tomas</a> was established in 1611. After Mexico became independent in 1821 the Spanish crown governed Manila directly, and banking, industry and education grew more than in the previous two centuries; the opening of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-canal">Suez Canal</a> in 1869 brought direct trade and communication with Spain, and bridges, roads, railways and ports were extended. Under American rule Daniel Burnham drew up a plan in 1905 for the city south of the Pasig. In 1960 Manila became the first Philippine city to exceed one million people, more than five times its population at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 1903.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>By the mid-16th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajah-sulayman">Rajah Sulayman</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajah-matanda">Rajah Matanda</a> ruled the Muslim communities south of the Pasig, which were joined as Maynila, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakandula">Lakandula</a> ruled Tondo to the north. Rajah Matanda was a great-grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolkiah">Sultan Bolkiah</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunei">Brunei</a>, and the two cities kept ties with the Bolkiah dynasty and with Sulu and Ternate. Earlier overlords are recorded: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a> texts list a territory called Seludong or Selurong, which scholars have identified with Manila, and a Majapahit conquest of the city in 1365 is also reported.</p><p>In 1570 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-de-goiti">Martín de Goiti</a> attacked Maynila for the Spanish governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-lopez-de-legazpi">Miguel López de Legazpi</a> and took it. In 1571 Legazpi returned with 280 Spaniards and 600 native allies, and the defenders set the city on fire and fled. Legazpi founded the Spanish city on its ruins, and Manila became the capital of the Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">East Indies</a> and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, which were administered under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">Viceroyalty of New Spain</a>, so that the governor-general in Manila answered to the viceroy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>. It was decreed the capital of the Philippines in 1595. British forces took the city in October 1762 and held it until 1764, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> governed it directly from 1821.</p><p>After the Battle of Manila of 1898 Spain ceded the city to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, which chartered it in 1901. Japanese forces entered it on 2 January 1942, and American and Philippine troops recaptured it in 1945. In 1948 the seat of government moved to Quezon City, and in 1976 President Marcos made Manila the capital again.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Manila&#8221; cites where it covers Spanish colonial era, American dominion era, Postwar and independence era, Martial Law era:</p><ul><li>José S. Arcilla, Re-shaping the world: Philip II of Spain and his time (Ateneo de Manila University Press, 2008)</li><li>José Roca de Togores y Saravia, Blockade and siege of Manila (National Historical Institute, 2003)</li><li>Cristina Evangelista Torres, The Americanization of Manila, 1898-1921 (UP Press, 2010)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/philippine-revolution"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philippine-revolution.jpg" alt="Philippine Revolution" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/philippine-revolution">Philippine Revolution</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:UltimosFilipinas.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Desconocido (pertenece a la misma sesión de fotos que File:Los defensores de Baler en Barcelona (septiembre de 1899, La …, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The city was divided by origin and function. Intramuros, the walled city, was built to keep out Chinese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> and to protect the city from native uprisings, and the Spanish confined the Chinese to a separate district, the Parian de Alcaceria, after the attack of 1574. Binondo, said to be the world’s oldest Chinatown, lay outside the walls, and Ermita took its name from a Mexican hermit who lived there. The Augustinians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franciscans">Franciscans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> and Dominicans founded schools and churches, and Manila followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunei">Brunei</a> in converting from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> by the 16th century, before the Spanish came.</p><p>The Spanish missionary Juan de Cobo wrote in the 1600s to his brethren in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> that the diversity of the city was immense: Castilians from every province, Portuguese, Italians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, Mexican Indians, African slaves, Javanese, Japanese and Bengalese, and Chinese whose numbers outnumbered everyone else. Burial statistics of the 1860s to 1890s give pure Spaniards as much as 3.3 per cent of the population, pure Chinese 9.9 per cent and mixed Spanish-Filipinos 19 per cent. The city’s growing wealth and education in the 19th century raised a class of ilustrados, whose liberal ideas became the ideological foundations of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippine-revolution">Philippine Revolution</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagalog-language">Tagalog</a> as spoken in Manila became the basis of Filipino, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingua-franca">lingua franca</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, while English is the language most used in education and business and Hokkien is spoken by the Chinese-Filipino community.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-marcos"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ferdinand-marcos.jpg" alt="Ferdinand Marcos" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-marcos">Ferdinand Marcos</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ferdinand_Marcos%27_official_portrait_during_his_second_term_(1969)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Malacañang Press, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Manila was fought over many times. In 1571 the defenders burned the city before the Spanish took it, and in 1574 the Chinese warlord Limahong attacked it with a force of about 3,000 men and 62 warships and was defeated. The Chinese revolted again in 1602, setting fire to Quiapo and Tondo, and in 1662; expulsions followed, though the community usually returned. The earthquakes of 1645 and 1677 destroyed the stone-and-brick city. After the British capture of 1762 the city was pillaged for 40 hours.</p><p>In December 1941 American and Filipino forces were ordered to withdraw from the city, which was declared an open city, but Japanese aircraft still bombed it. From 3 February to 3 March 1945 Manila was the site of one of the bloodiest battles of the Pacific war. General Yamashita ordered the destruction of the bridges and the evacuation of the city, but units of the Japanese navy under Rear Admiral Sanji Iwabuchi refused to leave. Japanese forces killed civilians in what is called the Manila massacre; estimates of the civilians killed range from about 100,000 to 500,000, and women were mass raped in February. Heavy bombardment, American as well, destroyed most of the city, the second most destroyed of the war after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-marcos">Ferdinand Marcos</a> declared martial law in September 1972, and about 70,000 people eventually became political detainees. After a disputed election in February 1986 a crowd gathered on Epifanio de los Santos Avenue to protect two camps held by defecting officers, and on 26 February Marcos and his family fled the Malacañang Palace.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jose-rizal"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jose-rizal.jpg" alt="José Rizal" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jose-rizal">José Rizal</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jose_Rizal_full.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Manila’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galleon">galleon</a> trade has been called the first to connect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-america">Spanish Americas</a> across the Pacific, and the city, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, has been counted among the world’s original set of global cities. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippine-revolution">Philippine Revolution</a> grew in the city: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-rizal">José Rizal</a>’s novel Noli Me Tangere was published in 1886, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andres-bonifacio">Andrés Bonifacio</a> founded the Katipunan in Tondo, and Rizal was executed at Bagumbayan on 30 December 1896.</p><p>The war destroyed almost all of Manila’s pre-war and Spanish colonial architecture. Some buildings were rebuilt, among them the Old Legislative Building, now the National Museum of Fine Arts, and many historic churches and buildings in Intramuros were damaged beyond repair. San Agustin Church, within Intramuros, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. The writer Nick Joaquin described Manila as a city of layers, the Manila of the rajahs, the conquistadors, the viceroys and the Commonwealth, each destroyed and buried before the next rose on the same site.</p><p>The Burnham Plan was never completed, and the move of the government to Quezon City in 1948 ended any plan for the government centre at Luneta. Metro Manila was created as a unit in 1975, with four cities and thirteen towns. The city proper had 1,902,590 inhabitants at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 2024, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pasig-river">Pasig River</a> still divides it into northern and southern parts.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/o-canada">O Canada<span>1880</span></a><a href="/songs/bayan-ko">Bayan Ko<span>1929</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Manila, on Manila Bay in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagalog-language">Tagalog</a> settlement by the 13th century and the Spanish capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">East Indies</a> from 1571, the western end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galleon">galleon</a> trade to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acapulco">Acapulco</a>. Held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> from 1762 to 1764 and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> from 1898, it was largely destroyed in 1945 and rebuilt as the capital of the Philippines.</em></p>

